{
  "case_id": "2010-09-571",
  "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This case asks when a district attorney's office may be liable under section 1983 for inadequately training prosecutors. The Petitioner, Orleans Parish District Attorney's Office, was found liable for the terrible injuries caused to Mr. Thompson by a Brady violation on the theory the office was deliberately indifferent to Brady training, this despite the fact that there was proved no pattern of previous misconduct by office prosecutors."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0000-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The district court exempted this case from the ordinary pattern requirement by making a flawed analogy to a hypothetical in this Court's City of Canton opinion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Isn't this something in between? Because in Canton, the hypothetical was one rookie police officer. Here, it wasn't one rogue prosecutor. There were four prosecutors who knew of this blood evidence, and there were multiple opportunities for them to disclose it, but four of them apparently thought it was okay under Brady to keep this quiet."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0000-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There is no question that, whether it was one or four prosecutors, this is a single incident of a Brady violation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Duncan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0001-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DUNCAN: What was -- the Fifth Circuit panel in this case affirmatively said Thompson did not even try to prove a pattern, and he did not prove a pattern of violations. The Fifth Circuit panel said that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Did you -- did you object to it? To this charge?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Wait a minute. Wait a minute. What evidence is there that they put this in their hip pocket?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cooney",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COONEY: Number one, there was absolutely no Brady training at all."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Could I ask you what -- most law offices with which I'm familiar, the training is mentoring. In other words, the young attorneys learn from the older attorneys, often by following them along -- around. Would it have been an adequate training program for this office simply to say, new prosecutors, you don't get to be first chair prosecutors until after a year, and you're going to follow one of the prosecutors around and learn from them? Is that an adequate training program?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Is that consistent with Arizona v. Youngblood?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cooney",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0002-0003",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COONEY: Your Honor, four prosecutors -- it is clear that four prosecutors knew about the existence of blood evidence for months, and it was never produced to the defense. And that blood evidence would have conclusively established John Thompson's innocence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: But what do you do with the Dubelier testimony? Didn't he testify that it was standard operating procedure to turn over all lab reports?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Stuart K. Duncan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0002-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "REBUTTAL ARGUMENT OF STUART K. DUNCAN ON BEHALF OF THE PETITIONERS"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito and Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What would you tell a ADAs with respect to training -- and I believe Justice Sotomayor as well -- that would have prevented such a thing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Duncan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Whatever caused the violation, I haven't heard about a specific training measure that would have actually prevented what happened in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "How do you train your new hires? First day, somebody right out of law school shows up and says, I want to be an assistant district attorney. How do you train them?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Cooney",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the first thing you do is you have a hiring process that emphasizes the important of -- the importance of Brady, as this office did. Brady was important. One witness said -- McElroy -- from the moment you walked in the door, you had to write an essay on Brady. Brady was emphasized as being very important."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Duncan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0003-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The jury found that that was inadequate."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0003-0006",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the jury could have found, a reasonable jury could have found, that there was no training here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0003-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So we have to overturn the jury finding?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Duncan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0003-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "No, Your Honor. Absolutely not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Duncan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0003-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "we don't contest the finding of inadequate training. What we contest is the ingredients that lead -- that can lead to a deliberate indifference finding on the basis of inadequate training."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Duncan",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0003-0010",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The correct resolution is the lower court should dismiss the failure-to-train claim as a matter of law because there was no demonstration of a pattern of violations, and this situation does not fall within the narrow range of circumstances that Canton foresees for single-incident liability."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0003-0011",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-571",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6ae201a22e7c0c0759674a09ab7d801ac94829347bc3f4eb4769a4db5410beaa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6ae201a22e7c-0003-0012",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "(Whereupon, at 12:05 p.m., the case in the above-entitled matter was submitted.)"
    },
